### What is the length of a linked list?

The length of a linked list is the number of nodes it contains. Since a linked list does not store its size as a property by default, you typically compute it by traversing the entire chain and counting each node.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanNode {
    String loanId;
    BigDecimal amount;
    LoanNode next;

    public LoanNode(String loanId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.loanId = loanId;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

### How do you count nodes by traversal?

Set a counter to zero and a `current` pointer to the head. While `current` is not null, increment the counter and advance to the next node. When the loop ends, the counter holds the length.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.LoanNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanListLength {
    public static int countNodes(LoanNode head) {
        int count = 0;
        LoanNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            count++;
            current = current.next;
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LoanNode head = new LoanNode("LOAN-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        head.next = new LoanNode("LOAN-002", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        head.next.next = new LoanNode("LOAN-003", new BigDecimal("200000"));

        System.out.println("Number of pending loans: " + countNodes(head));
    }
}
```

```text
Number of pending loans: 3
```

### How do you maintain a size field for O(1) length?

Instead of counting every time, you can store a `size` field in the list object and update it on every mutation. This is how `java.util.LinkedList` works internally.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.LoanNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanLinkedList {
    private LoanNode head;
    private int size;

    public LoanLinkedList() {
        this.head = null;
        this.size = 0;
    }

    public void addFirst(String loanId, BigDecimal amount) {
        LoanNode node = new LoanNode(loanId, amount);
        node.next = head;
        head = node;
        size++; // must update on every add
    }

    public LoanNode removeFirst() {
        if (head == null) return null;
        LoanNode removed = head;
        head = head.next;
        removed.next = null;
        size--; // must update on every remove
        return removed;
    }

    public int getSize() {
        return size; // O(1)
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LoanLinkedList list = new LoanLinkedList();
        list.addFirst("LOAN-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        list.addFirst("LOAN-002", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        list.addFirst("LOAN-003", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        System.out.println("Size (O(1)): " + list.getSize());

        list.removeFirst();
        System.out.println("After remove: " + list.getSize());
    }
}
```

```text
Size (O(1)): 3
After remove: 2
```

### How is traversal count different from maintained size?

| Feature | Traversal Count | Maintained Size |
|---|---|---|
| Time to read length | O(n) | O(1) |
| Extra storage | None | One int field |
| Accuracy guarantee | Always correct | Correct only if every mutation updates it |
| Code complexity | Simple one-time scan | Every add/remove must update the field |
| Use case | Rare length checks | Frequent length checks |

```java
// Traversal count: always accurate, always O(n)
int count = 0;
LoanNode current = head;
while (current != null) { count++; current = current.next; }

// Maintained size: O(1) but requires discipline
int size = list.getSize(); // instant
```

### What happens if the maintained size drifts out of sync?

If you forget to update the size field in any mutation path, the count silently drifts. In a financial system this is a serious bug because reports that depend on the count will show wrong numbers.

```java
// BUG: forgot to decrement size on remove
public LoanNode removeFirstBuggy() {
    if (head == null) return null;
    LoanNode removed = head;
    head = head.next;
    removed.next = null;
    // size-- is missing!
    return removed;
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: always update size on every mutation
public LoanNode removeFirstCorrect() {
    if (head == null) return null;
    LoanNode removed = head;
    head = head.next;
    removed.next = null;
    size--;
    return removed;
}
```

### Can you count nodes recursively?

Yes, but iterative is preferred for production because the recursive version uses O(n) stack frames and risks `StackOverflowError` on long lists.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.LoanNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RecursiveCount {
    public static int countRecursive(LoanNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        return 1 + countRecursive(node.next);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LoanNode head = new LoanNode("LOAN-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        head.next = new LoanNode("LOAN-002", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        head.next.next = new LoanNode("LOAN-003", new BigDecimal("200000"));

        System.out.println("Recursive count: " + countRecursive(head));
    }
}
```

```text
Recursive count: 3
```

### What are the edge cases?

The empty list and single-node list are the primary edge cases. The traversal count handles both naturally because the loop either never runs (empty) or runs exactly once (single node).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.LoanNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CountEdgeCases {
    public static int count(LoanNode head) {
        int c = 0;
        LoanNode cur = head;
        while (cur != null) { c++; cur = cur.next; }
        return c;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + count(null));
        System.out.println("Single: " + count(new LoanNode("L-1", new BigDecimal("1000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: 0
Single: 1
```

### How can you audit the maintained size?

In production, you can use the traversal count as an audit tool to verify that the maintained size field has not drifted. Run a periodic check that compares the two values.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.LoanNode;

public class SizeAuditor {
    public static boolean auditSize(LoanNode head, int maintainedSize) {
        int actualCount = 0;
        LoanNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            actualCount++;
            current = current.next;
        }
        if (actualCount != maintainedSize) {
            System.out.println("DRIFT DETECTED: maintained=" + maintainedSize
                + " actual=" + actualCount);
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that `java.util.LinkedList.size()` is O(1) because it maintains a count internally. When building a custom linked list for a specific use case, the choice between traversal count and maintained size depends on how often the length is queried versus how many mutation paths exist. More mutation paths mean more places where a forgotten update could cause drift.

In concurrent environments, a maintained size must be updated atomically along with the structural change, or a race condition could make the count incorrect. Using `AtomicInteger` or synchronization is necessary in multithreaded code.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class ThreadSafeLoanList {
    private volatile LoanNode head;
    private final AtomicInteger size = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public synchronized void addFirst(String id, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        LoanNode node = new LoanNode(id, amount);
        node.next = head;
        head = node;
        size.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public int getSize() {
        return size.get();
    }
}
```
